        {
            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the issue!\n\nI can indeed reproduce with the basic 'blink.cmp' and 'mini.git' setup, but it doesn't happen with 'mini.completion' and 'mini.snippets'.\n\nWhat happens in the screencast is 'blink.cmp' accepts snippet entry from friendly-snippets for 'gitcommit' filetype and something strange happens. Accepting the exact same snippet with 'mini.completion' and 'mini.snippets' does not have the same behavior. I used the following setup for them:\n\n```lua\nrequire('mini.completion').setup()\n\nlocal snippets = require('mini.snippets')\nsnippets.setup({ snippets = { snippets.gen_loader.from_lang() } })\nMiniSnippets.start_lsp_server()\n```\n\nSo this makes me believe that the issue is somewhere on the 'blink.cmp' side. My guess is that it looks like a side effect of workarounds it has to do to support some features (like dot-repeat). This indeed might be related to how the commit buffer is opened: it is opened as a result of `GIT_EDITOR` environment variable at work. Here are some pointers:\n- The `GIT_EDITOR` is [set to open a split in the current isntance](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/77e20cd6ed80fdf1738c116c35da81e7d9ad997e/lua/mini/git.lua#L689-L694) via \n- The split is opened as a result of a [convoluted `nvim --clean` which \"talks\" to the current instance](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/77e20cd6ed80fdf1738c116c35da81e7d9ad997e/lua/mini/git.lua#L803-L830).\n\nClosing as it doesn't look like a 'mini.git' issue. If there is a relatively concise and non-intrusive way for it to make the use case with 'blink.cmp' work, I'll take a look. But finding the route cause in 'blink.cmp' itself is a bit too much for me.",
